#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097608} :activity/id #uuid "ccadebeb-ab4f-5e8c-b59d-112db894f229" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049885 :topic/id #uuid "1b27fe0a-58ff-57f2-8e77-9341dd7fc153" :topic/math-academy-id 1082} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097796 :tutorial/content "The region enclosed by the curves $y=x^4$ and $y=x$ is shown below. Suppose that this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the horizontal line $y=-1,$ generating a solid of revolution. How can we write an integral expression that gives the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/18/1880138e4a20cb5a47ba89cb3177d5dc6e74f998d7fdd428cdded7af38cadd02.png)\n\nFirst, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =x \\\\ {x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/49/49da0885bbf23e586b488e2b2a9bcaa0d923d3591833c9b8beb31a611c1d8b43.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x^4,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $y=x.$\n\nNow, we can express the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves, as follows:\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/35/352ff26bb5dce0a923c102c4528bd89555b49203c212fb73a42cf31cdf8b226e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(x)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x^4.$\n\n![](images/ee/ee0fa31b61acd4a1f2a9b6bb21dcd0e5eed3c529780fcb67096d40dccf90c20c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,({x}^{4})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[x+1]}^{2}-{[{x}^{4}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8e33de60-9df4-540e-b19b-f12088b349e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1064 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "626bee34-cf24-50f9-b6b3-1fcbce8fe1a3" :step/math-academy-id 638288 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096531 :question/id #uuid "b6dd2970-3cdc-58fc-8f28-0bfdcd738d6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-593"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545802}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "52be5c09-e883-51da-a981-64e4b7986356" :step/math-academy-id 638292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097797 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\dfrac x2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the vertical line $x=-2.$ Let's find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/78/7876211e2781a079ac2444436e671e2d61c28ec09774c0acad65e96fb537927a.png)\n\nFirst, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{x}{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=2y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y={2}.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3fd84335240208fb158da9b67aaffa07cf490c67f8a77bf28947df998180b76.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y^2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=2y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2y.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a075f9b5fb64923de7037b31ec6dd0408e88aa6d965ebb8b204b6c560e23ec1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(2y)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[2y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cca4c498956a16cb374a8d0b3341a09e52844026593557710525d07be79a233a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,({y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[2y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[2y+2]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{y}^{4}+8y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}+4{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{32}{5}+16)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "78a4b0ee-944d-5de3-846a-bf591d05387b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1066 :tutorial/title "Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "ca0b12a1-0e89-5062-b87e-358443f928e2" :step/math-academy-id 638295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096532 :question/id #uuid "b79b0d88-0b42-5223-927b-5a4023f6c329" :question/math-academy-id "e-594"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545873}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "bb053558-c7a0-533b-bcf7-e8427cfa707a" :step/math-academy-id 638299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096569 :question/id #uuid "8e436e4c-45da-528c-885b-9a094919a652" :question/math-academy-id "e-7508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048643}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "38143ed9-ca20-5c45-b750-274568e87032" :step/math-academy-id 638289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096570 :question/id #uuid "d064d066-3bbe-5381-ab56-3fc407da144f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545814}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "3a272f90-abf1-569a-853d-f9c7cfbd0534" :step/math-academy-id 638296 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097611}}] :activity/title "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096871}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545916} :activity/id #uuid "43c709a2-005f-55fb-9b67-4db81bb9fadf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049885 :topic/id #uuid "1b27fe0a-58ff-57f2-8e77-9341dd7fc153" :topic/math-academy-id 1082} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d266feb7-a04f-5c98-9303-8d8350d33397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29\\pi}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545582} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6753ef3-ae19-5502-968e-b02e543e893c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1313560c-b0d0-5277-bf49-6e697b836fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-350910" :question/problem "![](images/87/87481d3de1bd3fb02a2fbaa344f120421052335624e8d42b6dd853382b9f764e.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/bd/bd558d08aa48a17d04b9c3929a5b49a21e5fd7bde8ebfe4a07f8cabfd824b981.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8c8e0216a8ee045a9d7a570bc26d1765fc54e2110b1c1d2fbbc8f59d1ec22ef.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\sqrt{x}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/4c/4cd4e1fb0ad340da9a2e146fa261c6a50e4578f1b77564c8d6a5c6a2f27c81f9.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,({x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\sqrt{x}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(\\sqrt{x}+1)}^{2}-{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{4}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}(1)^{5}-\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{29\\pi }{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09990606-8461-5b73-b77b-371381bbc6e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ea9e3-bd8d-542f-b149-f78e0b396574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{252\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545249} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ca58cc0-f38a-5004-9064-b0da06a85013" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f01f287c-3406-5e67-8ee1-f944f0c8187c" :question/math-academy-id "q-361835" :question/problem "![](images/35/353b6ad37a77d200719a155e8c14a974f01de00a2e4b4ca3312ce50401a715b7.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+2$ and $y=x-4$ for $y\\in [-1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical axis, we need to solve the equation of our second curve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-4\\,⟹\\,x=y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7e/7e08a30f9ef1f8ce748ba0fcac73f1e93d994e4fd3a3d3d1367568c89c29f4d0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y+4.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y+4.$\n\n![](images/26/262546bf3f1d1c9025d23b9fb134c9de7b5299c7b6383563531dffa62244163d.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(y+4)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[y+6]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/ce/cefab13ca3d09fcf596e7906384c2efd1718c289783792f1b0ff950632c823ad.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({y}^{2}+2)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+4]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of our solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[y+6]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+4]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({y}^{2}+12y+36-({y}^{4}+8{y}^{2}+16))\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}(12y+20-{y}^{4}-7{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(6{y}^{2}+20y-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [\\,(6\\cdot {2}^{2}+20\\cdot 2-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {2}^{3}}{3})\\,-\\,(6-20+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{584}{15}-(-\\frac{172}{15})) \\\\  & =\\frac{252\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "44438865-e406-5603-858e-c1b84ef5f87e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aa037eb-acb4-59c7-9bc8-b5a91aeab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-6}^{-1}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545289} {:answer/id #uuid "73772f39-b6a2-53e5-a227-0d97808f09d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545417} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9e7748-eb16-59b5-abdd-e671cdbf6651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[7-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545488} {:answer/id #uuid "d032dc32-0433-5a8b-bab4-f8aeb0c6d4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545577} {:answer/id #uuid "ebffd986-5bc8-51e2-b270-81cdea5769e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545577} :answer-field/id #uuid "c103ad26-447b-5ac7-aeb2-5c94016982cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08b6d6f3-0fab-52e6-98e5-6de56edc9807" :question/math-academy-id "q-50331" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d930c097ed0ddfa454d7f443112c2d9e233e29521e8950fcc4738e742dd3d8dc.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{6}{y}$ and $x=7-y$ for $y\\in [1,6]$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-3.$  Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ed/edea788893771f13f3afc7d9f49e8f1dead4e9b589ca78ef160b9bddccf867c5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{6}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=7-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=7-y.$\n\n![](images/48/48df22796616a48f9e9ed56474ced12392905e976fdec064b401894f30cbf49b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(7-y)-(-3)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[10-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{6}{y}.$\n\n![](images/f3/f3240bb3d94231ae5b786b01b2fe11f9e0ea7971978e54f26a4e36b84af71a28.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\,(\\frac{6}{y})-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[10-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4571ed8d-5215-550d-9af8-82bcd85576c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0c35fb-e6e8-5a53-a69f-4b99e754b3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545293} {:answer/id #uuid "637dca43-26dc-54b2-8acd-b44b28e36455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545387} {:answer/id #uuid "66837ddd-4434-54af-8776-16b6edb795ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[y-7]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545395} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c46529-231a-5bc3-89ad-ebb9317619d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545626} {:answer/id #uuid "f97511ec-a987-529f-a0fa-f202424d84c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545387} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a1a3e4-d370-5a47-b2e6-255c12ad4fca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "423623d2-e3d9-5c6f-a470-2f2a329c961b" :question/math-academy-id "q-50428" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5ea4fcb15db81b35a8a6e399cc3df734fc1dd41afe42799b8ca2a39734996cb.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=-\\frac{10}{y}$ and $x=y-7$ for $y\\in [2,5]$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/98/987999e84c2b22c8b90ecb0381518e7504d0b792ecebb067a7221653422b3e89.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=-\\frac{10}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y-7.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y-7.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8c250c5fbf20980050a1e86f4ac5c91bff7b24bfc7ce5394f33c87703139026.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}[\\,(-1)-(y-7)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[6-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=-\\frac{10}{y}.$\n\n![](images/64/64b440be918c616eb07f05f7f09cb8e14ed649f677afc5f40f66f47ec34ed87e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[\\,(-1)-(-\\frac{10}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[6-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "51e6f5a0-33a0-5933-a201-79b857fc27c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107da7cc-1b85-5247-b8b7-0e025ec0a5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{153\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545228} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6b62faf-4750-56ae-8c93-f35beee1fd2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6932dce0-68da-5dc2-a092-7a3088f2337d" :question/math-academy-id "q-361838" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce9f6280200d6f71fc13b4dba705c26341ddf11d369b547e05c8647c0e1c3e8e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}-2$ and $x=y$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2 & =y \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=2.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/74/74e298b85aa9e300d303fa34c761d5ffad214110e315ccf18c33edd2283210ad.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}-2.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/94/945dfc9ade4dc95a336f6c0111ea50496242ca60dfddff61f85961547c0c7d3e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2}-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[5-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](images/9e/9e25b8fd45f666927edbfec7d9ccd714d14037784f86fb9a8493bf861d521ff9.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[3-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[5-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[3-y]}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}((25-10{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})-(9-6y+{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({y}^{4}-11{y}^{2}+6y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{11{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2}+16y)|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{11({2}^{3})}{3}+3({2}^{2})+16(2))-(-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{11}{3}+3-16)] \\\\  & =\\pi [\\frac{316}{15}-(-\\frac{143}{15})] \\\\  & =\\frac{153\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e492624e-2375-5b7b-b58f-3032d0f7b274"}] :activity/title "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545187}]}
